{% comment %}
  AI review summary — "What buyers say".

  Rendered server-side from an app-owned JSON metafield, exactly like the review
  list. That is the whole point: every competitor injects their summary with
  JavaScript, so Google and the AI shopping assistants never see the text. Ours
  is in the initial HTML response, which makes the same code both a conversion
  feature and an AEO one (PLAN.md §5.3).

  Every line here is backed by a mention count computed from real reviews. The
  job that writes this metafield drops any point fewer than two reviewers
  raised, so there is no path by which this block renders a claim about a
  merchant's product that nobody actually made (PLAN.md §4.2 — the liability
  boundary).

  Parameters:
    summary   — parsed `app.summary` metafield value
    uid       — block id, for the aria-describedby wiring

  Renders nothing at all when the metafield is absent, empty, or has been
  cleared — a Free store, a product under the review threshold, and a corpus
  with no consensus are all correctly indistinguishable here.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign has_pros = false
  assign has_cons = false
  assign has_themes = false
  assign has_highlights = false

  if summary.highlights != blank and summary.highlights.size > 0
    assign has_highlights = true
  endif

  if summary.pros != blank and summary.pros.size > 0
    assign has_pros = true
  endif
  if summary.cons != blank and summary.cons.size > 0
    assign has_cons = true
  endif
  if summary.themes != blank and summary.themes.size > 0
    assign has_themes = true
  endif
-%}

{%- if has_pros or has_cons or has_themes or has_highlights -%}
  <div class="cited-summary" role="group" aria-labelledby="cited-summary-title-{{ uid }}">
    <h3 class="cited-summary__title" id="cited-summary-title-{{ uid }}">What buyers say</h3>

    {%- comment -%}
      Stated plainly rather than as a badge. A shopper deciding whether to trust
      a summary is entitled to know a machine wrote it and what it read.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <p class="cited-summary__provenance">
      Summarised from this product's reviews. Counts show how many reviewers
      raised each point.
    </p>

    {%- if has_pros or has_cons -%}
      <div class="cited-summary__columns">
        {%- if has_pros -%}
          <div class="cited-summary__column">
            <h4 class="cited-summary__column-title cited-summary__column-title--pro">Praised</h4>
            <ul class="cited-summary__points" role="list">
              {%- for point in summary.pros limit: 5 -%}
                <li class="cited-summary__point">
                  <span class="cited-summary__label">{{ point.label | escape }}</span>
                  <span class="cited-summary__mentions">
                    {%- if point.mentions == 1 -%}
                      1 mention
                    {%- else -%}
                      {{ point.mentions }} mentions
                    {%- endif -%}
                  </span>
                </li>
              {%- endfor -%}
            </ul>
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- if has_cons -%}
          <div class="cited-summary__column">
            <h4 class="cited-summary__column-title cited-summary__column-title--con">Criticised</h4>
            <ul class="cited-summary__points" role="list">
              {%- for point in summary.cons limit: 5 -%}
                <li class="cited-summary__point">
                  <span class="cited-summary__label">{{ point.label | escape }}</span>
                  <span class="cited-summary__mentions">
                    {%- if point.mentions == 1 -%}
                      1 mention
                    {%- else -%}
                      {{ point.mentions }} mentions
                    {%- endif -%}
                  </span>
                </li>
              {%- endfor -%}
            </ul>
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      ── Review highlights ──
      Verbatim spans, each checked server-side against the review it claims to
      come from before it was ever stored — a paraphrase here would be words
      put in a named shopper's mouth on the merchant's storefront, so anything
      that failed that check never reaches this template.

      <blockquote> and <cite> rather than styled <div>s: these ARE quotations
      with attribution, and the semantics are what a screen reader and a
      crawler read.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if has_highlights -%}
      <div class="cited-summary__highlights">
        <h4 class="cited-summary__column-title">In their words</h4>
        {%- for highlight in summary.highlights limit: 4 -%}
          <blockquote class="cited-summary__quote">
            <p>{{ highlight.quote | escape }}</p>
            <cite class="cited-summary__quote-by">
              {{ highlight.author | default: 'Verified reviewer' | escape }}
              {%- if highlight.rating != blank -%}
                <span class="cited-summary__quote-rating">
                  {%- render 'cited-stars', rating: highlight.rating, scale_max: 5, size: 12 -%}
                </span>
              {%- endif -%}
            </cite>
          </blockquote>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if has_themes -%}
      <div class="cited-summary__themes">
        <h4 class="cited-summary__column-title">By theme</h4>
        <ul class="cited-summary__theme-list" role="list">
          {%- for theme in summary.themes limit: 6 -%}
            {%- liquid
              # The meter is a visual restatement of `sentiment`, never the
              # source of truth — the accessible name below carries the word
              # itself, so a screen reader is not asked to interpret dots.
              case theme.sentiment
                when 'very_positive'
                  assign filled = 5
                  assign sentiment_label = 'strongly positive'
                when 'positive'
                  assign filled = 4
                  assign sentiment_label = 'mostly positive'
                when 'mixed'
                  assign filled = 3
                  assign sentiment_label = 'mixed'
                when 'negative'
                  assign filled = 2
                  assign sentiment_label = 'mostly negative'
                when 'very_negative'
                  assign filled = 1
                  assign sentiment_label = 'strongly negative'
                else
                  assign filled = 3
                  assign sentiment_label = 'mixed'
              endcase
            -%}
            <li class="cited-summary__theme">
              <span class="cited-summary__theme-name">{{ theme.name | escape }}</span>
              <span
                class="cited-summary__meter"
                role="img"
                aria-label="{{ sentiment_label }}, {{ theme.mentions }} mentions"
              >
                {%- for i in (1..5) -%}
                  {%- if i <= filled -%}
                    <span class="cited-summary__dot cited-summary__dot--on" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                  {%- else -%}
                    <span class="cited-summary__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                  {%- endif -%}
                {%- endfor -%}
              </span>
              <span class="cited-summary__theme-note">{{ sentiment_label }}</span>
            </li>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </ul>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
